          <a href="#forecast" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Reading the Forecast</a>
          <a href="#time-of-day" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. The Best Time of Day</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Deck stain does not care what the afternoon high is. It cares about the temperature of the boards while it cures, the moisture in the air overnight, and whether it had time to harden before the next rain. Those three things are what a good staining day actually means.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            In most climates, <strong>late spring and early autumn</strong> are the two best windows. Daytime temperatures are moderate, overnight lows are still comfortably above the minimum, humidity is usually reasonable, and the sun is not intense enough to bake the stain off the surface before it can soak in.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Conditions to Aim For</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">50–90°F day, overnight low above 50°F, humidity under about 85%, and two dry days on each side.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Work in the shade rather than the sun, follow it around the deck as the day goes on, and check the overnight forecast as carefully as the daytime one. Those conditions are available far more often than people assume — they are just not the days that feel most like deck weather.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Midsummer is entirely workable, but it becomes a scheduling exercise: you follow the shade, start early, and accept that the sunniest section may have to wait until evening. High summer is also the season when people most want to use the deck, which is its own argument for doing the job in May or September instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Temperature, and the Number Nobody Checks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most deck stains specify a minimum application temperature of about 50 degrees Fahrenheit, and the important detail is that the limit applies to the whole cure period, not just the moment of application. A day that peaks at 72 and falls to 43 overnight spends most of the curing window below the minimum.
          </p>

          <p>
            What goes wrong differs by chemistry. Water-based stains rely on coalescence — the resin particles merging into a continuous film as the water leaves — and below the minimum film-forming temperature that process simply does not complete, leaving a weak finish that fails early. Oil-based stains cure by oxidation, which slows dramatically in the cold, so the coating stays soft and vulnerable for far longer than the label suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the other end, above about 90 degrees the problem reverses. The carrier evaporates too quickly, the stain sets before it can penetrate, and the working time shrinks so much that lap marks become almost unavoidable on long boards. Some manufacturers publish a narrower range than the general 50 to 90, and a few offer low-temperature formulations that go into the 30s — read the specific can rather than relying on the rule.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="humidity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Humidity, Dew and Why Autumn Nights Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Humidity affects the job twice. Before you stain, it controls how long the deck takes to dry after washing — a wash that dries in a day in dry air can take three in humid conditions. After you stain, it slows evaporation and extends both drying and cure times, dramatically so for water-based products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above roughly 85 percent relative humidity, most manufacturers recommend waiting. That is not an arbitrary threshold: at that point the air is close enough to saturation that little moisture leaves the coating, and an oil-based stain applied in those conditions can stay tacky for days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dew is the version of this problem that catches people out, and it is worst in autumn. On a clear night the deck surface radiates heat to the sky and can cool below the air temperature, dropping below the dew point and collecting a layer of moisture on a coating that has not finished curing. The effect is the same as light rain. This is why the overnight low and the dew point are worth checking alongside the daytime forecast, and it is covered further in <a href="/deck-stain-drying-time/">deck stain drying times</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sun" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sun and Hot Boards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Air temperature and board temperature are different numbers, and the second one is the one that matters. A dark deck in full sun on an 85-degree day can reach a surface temperature well over 120, and stain applied to it flashes off almost immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two problems follow. The stain never penetrates properly, so instead of soaking into the fibres it dries as a thin surface layer with much less durability. And the working time collapses, so the wet edge sets before you reach the end of a board, which is precisely the condition that produces lap marks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical answer is to follow the shade rather than the clock. Start on whichever side is shaded in the morning, move as the sun does, and leave the last section until it comes out of direct sun in the late afternoon. If you can rest your hand comfortably on the boards, the temperature is workable; if you cannot, wait. On a fully exposed deck, an overcast day is a genuine gift.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Rain Window on Both Sides
          </h2>

          <p>
            You need dry weather before staining and after it, and people tend to plan only for the second. The before-window exists because wood must be genuinely dry to accept stain: 24 hours after a light wash, 48 or more after a pressure wash, and longer in cool or humid conditions. Rain two days before you stain can be as damaging as rain two hours after.
          </p>

          <p>
            The after-window is about cure rather than dry. Most stains resist rain within a few hours in good conditions, but "resists rain" and "fully cured" are different states, and a heavy shower on a coating a few hours old can still leave dull patches or wash pigment out of an area. Aim for 24 to 48 hours of dry weather afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            If rain does catch a fresh coat, resist the urge to intervene immediately. Let the deck dry completely, then look at it in good light — a deck that looked ruined while wet often turns out to need nothing at all. Where a section genuinely was washed out, the fix is to clean and recoat that area once everything is properly dry, feathering the edges so the repair blends in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="seasons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Season by Season
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Season</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Early spring</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Marginal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold nights, wood still holding winter moisture</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Late spring</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spring rain patterns; pollen on the surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Midsummer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Workable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hot boards, short working time, afternoon storms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Early autumn</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Falling overnight lows; heavy dew on clear nights</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Late autumn</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Risky</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nights below 50, short drying days, leaf fall</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Winter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, in most climates</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Below minimum temperature for most of the cure</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Early spring is the trap on this list, because it feels like the right time — the deck looks tired after winter and the first warm weekend is inviting. But the wood has spent months absorbing moisture, the nights are still cold, and a coat applied then often fails within a season. Let the deck dry out for a few genuinely warm weeks first, and use the water test rather than the mood of the weekend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="forecast" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Reading the Forecast Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at four numbers rather than one. The daytime high tells you whether you can work; the overnight low tells you whether the coating can cure; the relative humidity tells you how long everything will take; and the precipitation outlook on both sides of the job tells you whether the wood will be dry going in and whether the coating survives coming out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The dew point is the fifth number and the one that most reliably explains a mysterious failure. When the overnight low approaches the dew point, expect condensation on the deck surface. A forecast low of 55 with a dew point of 54 means a wet deck by dawn, even under a clear sky with no rain in sight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put together, the ideal booking is a run of three or four settled days: one for cleaning, one or two for drying, and one for staining, with mild nights throughout and no rain until well after you have finished. That is a more demanding requirement than most people plan for, which is exactly why so many deck jobs get rushed into an unsuitable window.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="time-of-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Best Time of Day
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start once the dew has burned off, which in practice means mid-morning rather than first light. Staining over overnight moisture that has not evaporated puts water between the coating and the wood, with the same consequences as staining after rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aim to finish by mid to late afternoon so the coating gets several hours of warm, dry air before the temperature drops. Applying stain at six in the evening on a day that cools quickly gives the coating almost no useful curing time before dew forms, and this is one of the most common causes of a deck that is still tacky the next morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a large deck that cannot be finished in one session, split it by natural lines — a section, a level, the stairs — rather than stopping in the middle of a run of boards. Where you stop mid-board, the lap mark is permanent; where you stop at a joint, nobody will ever see it. The technique behind that is covered in <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/">how to stain a deck</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Regional Differences That Change the Window</h2>
        <p>
          In the humid southeast, the limiting factor is rarely temperature and almost always moisture. Summer afternoon thunderstorms are frequent enough that a morning start is close to mandatory, and the high ambient humidity extends both the drying time before staining and the cure time afterwards. Spring and autumn are both long and generous, which makes the timing less fraught than the summer suggests.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the arid southwest and at elevation, the problem is intensity rather than moisture. Ultraviolet exposure is high, surface temperatures on an exposed deck are extreme, and stain flashes off far faster than the label anticipates. Shade discipline matters more here than anywhere, early morning and evening work becomes normal, and the recoat interval is shorter than the national averages suggest.
        </p>
        <p>
          In northern and mountain climates the season is simply shorter. Late May through September is often the realistic window, spring decks hold winter moisture well into the warm weather, and the September temptation to squeeze a job in before winter runs directly into falling overnight lows. In the Pacific Northwest, the mid-summer dry spell is genuinely the best window despite the general advice to avoid high summer, because the rest of the year rarely gives four consecutive dry days.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">What Happens When You Stain at the Wrong Time</h2>
        <p>
          Staining too cold produces a coating that never fully hardens. A water-based product laid down below its minimum film-forming temperature stays chalky and weak and will wear away in a season; an oil-based one stays soft and tacky, picks up dirt, and shows every footprint. Neither can be rescued by waiting, because the cure that should have happened did not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Staining too hot, or in direct sun, produces the opposite failure: a thin surface layer that never penetrated, with visible lap marks where the wet edge set faster than you could work. It looks acceptable for a few weeks and then wears through unevenly, usually first in the traffic areas, leaving a patchy deck that needs attention far sooner than it should.
        </p>
        <p>
          Staining over damp wood is the most common of the three and the most consistently damaging. The stain cannot get into fibres already full of water, so it sits on top, dries blotchy, and lifts within a season. All three failures are indistinguishable to a homeowner at the time — they all look fine on the day — which is why the conditions matter more than they seem to while the deck is still wet with fresh stain.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Planning the Job Around the Weather</h2>
        <p>
          The most useful habit is to decouple the decision from the calendar. Rather than booking a weekend in advance and hoping, run the water test in spring to establish whether the deck is due at all, then watch for the first settled four-day stretch with mild nights. That approach usually lands the job in a better window than any date chosen a month ahead.
        </p>
        <p>
          Have everything bought and ready before that window appears, because the run of good days is not the time to be reading labels in a store. Cleaner, brightener, stain, brushes, and enough of the stain to finish the whole deck — running short mid-job is worse than waiting another week for the next window.
        </p>
        <p>
          And be prepared to abandon a session. If the boards are hotter than you expected, if the humidity climbs, or if the overnight forecast changes, stopping at a board joint and finishing another day costs you an afternoon. Pressing on costs you the job. Deciding that in advance makes it much easier to actually do when the moment comes.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Checking Only the Daytime High</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A warm afternoon means nothing if the night drops below the minimum, because the coating spends most of its cure in those hours. Water-based stains cannot form a proper film below about 50 degrees, and oil-based ones cure so slowly that dew lands on a soft surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining the First Warm Weekend of Spring</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The deck has absorbed moisture all winter and the nights are still cold, so the wood is not ready and the coating cannot cure. Let a few genuinely warm weeks pass first and let the water test, rather than the weather's mood, decide when to start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working in Direct Sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Board temperature, not air temperature, is what the stain experiences, and a dark deck in sun runs far hotter than the forecast. The stain flashes off before it penetrates and the wet edge sets faster than you can work, which guarantees lap marks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Dew Point</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a clear night a deck radiates heat and can cool below the air temperature, condensing moisture onto a coating that has not cured. A forecast low close to the dew point means a wet deck by morning even with no rain, which is the classic autumn failure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting Too Late in the Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stain applied in the early evening gets almost no warm, dry air before the temperature falls and dew forms. Finish by mid to late afternoon so the coating has several useful hours of curing, and start after the morning dew has burned off rather than at first light.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best time of year to stain a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Late spring and early autumn in most climates. Daytime temperatures are moderate, overnight lows stay above about 50 degrees, and the sun is not intense enough to flash the stain off before it penetrates. Midsummer works if you follow the shade around the deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature is too cold to stain a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most stains specify a minimum around 50 degrees Fahrenheit, and it applies to the overnight low as well as the daytime high. Below it, water-based products cannot form a proper film and oil-based ones cure so slowly that dew settles on an uncured surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain a deck in the fall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Early autumn is one of the best windows of the year — mild days, lower humidity, gentler sun. The risk is the overnight low, which falls quickly as the season goes on, and heavy dew on clear nights that acts like light rain on an uncured coating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to stain a deck in direct sunlight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Hot boards make the carrier evaporate before the stain can penetrate, so it dries as a thin surface layer, and the wet edge sets fast enough to produce lap marks. Follow the shade around the deck; if the boards are too hot to touch, wait.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should it be dry before and after staining a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 24 to 48 hours of dry weather before, so the wood is genuinely dry, and 24 to 48 hours after so the coating can cure. Pressure washing extends the before-window, and cool or humid conditions extend both.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain a deck in humid weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but everything slows down. High humidity lengthens drying and cure times substantially, particularly for water-based products, and the wood takes longer to dry after washing. Above roughly 85 percent relative humidity, most manufacturers advise waiting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it rains after you stain a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rain on an uncured coating can leave water spots, dull patches, or wash the stain out of an area. How bad it is depends on how long it had to cure: a shower after 48 hours usually does nothing, one after two hours may mean recoating that section.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is spring or fall better for staining a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both work well. Spring gives longer days and rising overnight temperatures, but the wood may still hold winter moisture. Autumn gives lower humidity and gentler sun but falling night temperatures and heavy dew, so its window closes faster than it appears to.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

