        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Late spring and early autumn are the best windows in most climates. Aim for a day between 50 and 90 degrees, an overnight low above 50, humidity below about 85 percent, and two dry days on each side of the job — the before-window matters as much as the after.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check the overnight low and the dew point, not just the afternoon high. Most of the cure happens after dark, and a clear autumn night that drops the deck below the dew point puts moisture on an uncured coating just as effectively as rain would.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then work in the shade and follow it around the deck. Board temperature, not air temperature, is what the stain experiences, and hot boards stop it penetrating and force lap marks. Start after the dew burns off, finish by late afternoon, and stop at a board joint rather than pressing on into bad conditions.
          </p>
